INTRO = "<p>These patents show, in the inventors' own drawings, how the main machines of a small power system are built: wind and water rotors, the dynamo, storage batteries, the motor and the lamp. Read them alongside the generator, battery and wiring chapters of this guide.</p>"
UNPATENTED = ["<b>Volta's pile</b> (1800), the first battery, and <b>Faraday's disc dynamo</b> (1831) were published as science and never patented &mdash; both are free to copy and are the simplest proofs of the principles.",
 "The basic <b>overshot and undershot water wheels</b> are ancient and unpatented; only later refinements such as the Pelton bucket below were patented."]
PATENTS = [
P("US1697574","d8/1e/90/c4544094f98bbb/US1697574.pdf","0c/0a/15/46fecc027ce54d/US1697574-drawings-page-1.png","A","1929-01-01","Sigurd J. Savonius","Rotor adapted to be driven by wind or flowing water",
  "The S-shaped vertical-axis rotor: two half-cylinders offset so wind pushing the concave side also spills through the gap onto the other blade. Slow but high-torque, self-starting and indifferent to wind direction &mdash; the classic rotor made from an oil drum cut in half.","Sheet 1: rotor plan view showing blade overlap; elevation of the rotor on its shaft"),
P("US233692","6a/19/4e/26608eb3abf576/US233692.pdf","a7/e0/8b/ce68afea7ea393/US233692-drawings-page-1.png","B","1880-10-26","Lester A. Pelton","Water-wheel",
  "The Pelton impulse wheel: a high-pressure jet strikes cups on the rim whose central ridge splits the water and turns it back, taking almost all its energy. Best for high head and small flow (a mountain stream in a pipe).","Bucket shape with central splitter; jet-to-bucket arrangement on the wheel"),
P("US1835018","13/d6/3b/c8f2897a86ce96/US1835018.pdf","10/dc/7f/87d8bca6f883de/US1835018-drawings-page-1.png","B","1931-12-08","Georges J. M. Darrieus","Turbine having its rotating shaft transverse to the flow of the current",
  "The Darrieus lift-type vertical-axis turbine: curved or straight airfoil blades on a vertical shaft that run faster than the wind. More efficient than the Savonius but not self-starting &mdash; often paired with a small Savonius to start it.","Sheet 1: blade layout around the shaft (curved 'eggbeater' and straight-blade forms)"),
P("US222881","23/2a/c8/51055a655b8000/US222881.pdf","cf/64/6c/030c4c87851002/US222881-drawings-page-1.png","B","1879-12-23","Thomas A. Edison","Improvement in magneto-electric machines",
  "An early practical dynamo: large field magnets and a rotating wound armature feeding a commutator, designed for low internal resistance so more of the power reaches the load. Shows the parts every DC generator needs.","Field magnet frame, armature and commutator arrangement"),
P("US678722","40/8f/51/506abac59d16cc/US678722.pdf","23/5f/59/430405b6450bc2/US678722-drawings-page-1.png","C","1901-07-16","Thomas A. Edison","Reversible galvanic battery",
  "Edison's nickel-iron alkaline storage cell: nickel and iron active materials in perforated steel pockets in potassium hydroxide electrolyte. Very long-lived and abuse-tolerant, but needs refined nickel and iron compounds.","Electrode plate with pockets; cell assembly"),
P("US381968","c9/47/b2/d2a511b818d8a6/US381968.pdf","80/2f/49/f4dd0854eee2ac/US381968-drawings-page-1.png","C","1888-05-01","Nikola Tesla","Electro-magnetic motor",
  "The AC induction motor: two alternating currents out of step create a rotating magnetic field that drags the rotor round with no brushes or commutator. Basis of almost every modern motor and of AC power distribution.","Sheet 1: field windings, generator connections and rotor (sheets 2&ndash;4 in the PDF show variants)"),
P("US223898","d4/9b/62/aac68f7e65536c/US223898.pdf","0d/fa/52/80e5cb24cdbab4/US223898-drawings-page-1.png","C","1880-01-27","Thomas A. Edison","Electric lamp",
  "The carbon-filament incandescent lamp: a high-resistance carbon filament sealed in an evacuated glass bulb with platinum lead-in wires. Needs glass-blowing and a good vacuum pump.","Filament shape, bulb, lead-in wires and seal"),
P("US64113","bb/d3/e7/05646c7b9a032c/US64113.pdf","83/08/05/50161dd31e8e84/US64113-drawings-page-1.png","A","1867-04-23","Georges Lionel Leclanch&eacute;","Improvement in combining generating and secondary or accumulating galvanic battery",
  "The Leclanch&eacute; cell, built in a jar. Put a carbon plate (for example gas-retort carbon) in a porous pot packed with powdered peroxide of manganese, or manganese mixed with graphite. Stand the pot in a wide-necked flask with a zinc plate, fill the flask with sand or sawdust, and wet everything with a strong solution of sal ammoniac (ammonium chloride). Carbon is the positive pole, zinc the negative.","The cell in section: porous pot, carbon, manganese packing, zinc and terminals"),
P("US466664","c0/26/14/851d73bce09aef/US466664.pdf","0f/32/db/8865e6e90a9478/US466664-drawings-page-1.png","A","1892-01-05","Patrick B. Delany","Electric battery",
  "An improved 'gravity' (crowfoot) cell: a copper plate lies on the bottom of a glass jar under copper-sulphate crystals, and a zinc 'crowfoot' hangs near the top. Delany's improvements fix the copper's lead wire so it cannot lift the plate, and add a sweep that scrapes deposits off the bottom of the zinc without taking the cell apart.","Fig. 1 the complete cell in section; Fig. 2 the zinc with its sweep"),
P("US11629","6d/be/48/fbfb1554fefa93/US11629.pdf","3d/8a/f3/864e23e6ab5ba9/US11629-drawings-page-1.png","B","1854-08-29","Daniel Halladay","Improved governor for wind mills",
  "A self-governing windmill. Each sail is mounted on its own turning spindle, and levers connect all the spindles to a sliding head on the main shaft. A governor slides the head so the sails turn edgewise as the wind rises, keeping the wheel at a steady speed. It is a way to keep a wind-driven pump or generator from over-speeding in a gale.","Fig. 1 side elevation with governor linkage; Fig. 2 face view of the sails"),
P("US266114","82/b7/34/dba8c453cb8518/US266114.pdf","9c/23/8d/eaa798eb54e60f/US266114-drawings-page-2.png","B","1882-10-17","Asahel K. Eaton","Secondary (storage) battery",
  "A lead-acid storage battery. The patent first explains the earlier designs. Planté rolled two lead sheets together, kept apart by rubber strips, in water with sulfuric acid, and charged them over and over, reversing the poles each time, until spongy lead built up on one sheet and lead peroxide on the other. That took weeks or months. Faure coated the plates with red lead oxide so that one charge was enough, but the cells were heavy. Eaton's plate is a thin lead skeleton (sheet or wire wickerwork) filled with fine spongy lead, to get the most surface for the least weight. Acid and charging gases are hazards; see 1.4 Safety and 2.2 Battery banks.","Fig. 1 top view of the cell; Figs. 2-3 sections; Fig. 4 the sponge-coated plate"),
]
